## Search paths

`TEXINPUTS` and `BIBINPUTS` (plus `BSTINPUTS`, `TFMFONTS`, `ENCFONTS`, passed on to `kpsewhich`) come from, first wins: the environment, the `latexmkrc` (`ensure_path`, `$ENV{…}`), the Makefile (`export`, inline), then `paths.*` in `satex.yaml`. kpathsea syntax applies (`:`, `//`, an empty component for the default). Relative entries resolve against the build's directory. `satex --version` shows each value and its source.

**Output directory.** With `$aux_dir`/`$out_dir`, `-outdir=` or `paths.output_directory`, relative names open there first (web2c `open_input`), so `build/main.aux` is read, not a stale copy.

## The clock

`clock` sets `\year`, `\month`, `\day`, `\time` and `\pdfcreationdate` (tex.web § 241).

| Value | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `now` (default) | the analysis time, reported once as `clock-assumed` |
| `2026-09-29`, `2026-09-29T14:30+02:00` | a pinned date |
| `unknown` | every digit unknown: date-dependent code is analyzed for every outcome |

A package reads the same clock as the document, as in a TeX job. A package cache is a function of what its load found, the clock and the job name among it:

- A value the load only stores is filled in where the cache is served. `\count@=\time`, arithmetic on it (`\advance`, `\multiply`, `\divide`), and `\the` of it, `\jobname` or `\pdfcreationdate` inside an `\edef` are stored as what they compute.
- A test on such a value (`\ifnum`) is stored with its outcome. The cache is served where the test comes out the same (pgfmath's random seed at any minute but midnight).
- Anything else that looks at the value keys that part of the load on the exact value. It is read again where the value differs.

One cache file serves every date and every job name. Document caches are kept per setting.

## Where paths join

Undecided tests part the run into paths ([Domains](domains.md)). They join where they stand alike, and at the points `analysis.join_paths_at` names.

```yaml
analysis:
  join_paths_at:
    - command: \document
    - hook: begindocument
```

{{fields: AnalysisConfig analysis}}

## Stubs

Stubs are a temporary exception. The goal is none. A stub stands in for a macro SaTeX cannot yet run. Each use is an `assumption`.

```yaml
stubs:
  \includegraphics: { args: "som", result: box }
  \xinttheexpr: { until: \relax, result: { text: number } }
```

`args`: `s` star, `o` optional, `m` mandatory, or `until` a delimiter. `result` is an element of the [unknown-text lattice](domains.md#unknown-text-the-textclass-lattice): `nothing`, `number` / `{ number: [lo, hi] }`, `dimen`, `text` / `{ text: digits | number | dimension | cs-free | any }`, `box` / `{ box: { width, height, depth } }`. `sets` names what it assigns (`sets: { \pgfmathresult: number }`). A document that redefines the name gets its own definition.

## Configurable limits for the analysis

| Key | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `limits.memory` | `8GiB` | stops the run with `budget-exhausted` (`--max-memory`), library users get no limit |
| `limits.checkpoint_memory` | `1GiB` | what the saved states for reruns may copy together, past it every other one goes. Results stay the same, reruns resume further back |
| `limits.checkpoint_steps` | `8388608` | steps between two saved states at first. Past `limits.checkpoints` of them every other one goes and the spacing doubles |
| `limits.checkpoints` | `8` | saved states a run keeps for its reruns, besides the ones around the last edit and where the document begins |
| `limits.checkpoint_body_share` | `8` | in the body a state is also saved between top-level lines after this fraction of `limits.checkpoint_steps` |
| `limits.checkpoints_after_edit` | `2` | states kept on the first lines past an edit and past the next pages shipped, where the next edit's run may meet this one |
| `limits.checkpoint_meet_steps` | `4194304` | after an edit far from every saved state the old text is run again to save states around it, at most this many steps past the edit. `0` never does |
| `limits.seconds` | `300` | stops the run, lints after it share the same budget |
| `limits.degrade_at_percent` | `100` (off) | past that share of `limits.steps`, halves the time-only limits, reported as `precision-degraded` |
| `limits.threads` | `0` (`min(4, cores)`) | file scans only, the interpreter is one thread |

`--max-steps`, `--max-memory` and `--threads` set the first, second and last for one run. Steps are counted alike on every machine, so results never depend on speed. Seconds and memory only stop a run. The installation's answers (`kpsewhich --expand-var`, engine version) are cached against the program's path and timestamp.
